 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Wide Radius Range:** If particle radii span several orders of magnitude, using $r_{max}$ causes the enlarged frustum to be excessively large. This results in traversing many empty cells and intersecting many unnecessary spheres, potentially outweighing the benefits of single storage [S1]. Frustum culling is typically required to mitigate this performance loss [S1].
-*   **Non-Uniform Radii:** The method assumes radii are uniform or within a small range. If this assumption is violated, the performance gain diminishes [S1, S2].
-*   **Plane Normal Definition:** The calculation of $s$ depends on the definition of $dU$. The sources define $dU$ as the direction vector of the given plane's normal in a reduced 2D context where the normal is $\langle -dU, 1 \rangle$. Implementers must ensure $dU$ is extracted correctly from the frustum plane definitions [S1, S2].
+*   **Wide Radius Range:** If particle radii span several orders of magnitude, using $r_{max}$ causes the enlarged frustum to be excessively large [S1]. This results in traversing many empty cells and intersecting many unnecessary spheres, potentially outweighing the benefits of single storage [S1]. Frustum culling is typically required to mitigate this performance loss [S1].
+*   **Non-Uniform Radii:** The method assumes radii are uniform or within a small range [S2]. If this assumption is violated, the performance gain diminishes [S1, S2].
+*   **Plane Normal Definition:** The calculation of $s$ depends on the definition of $dU$ [S1, S2]. The sources define $dU$ as the direction vector of the given plane's normal in a reduced 2D context where the normal is $\langle -dU, 1 \rangle$ [S1]. Implementers must ensure $dU$ is extracted correctly from the frustum plane definitions [S1, S2].
 
